<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Abadzekhs</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing Circassian people of the forested foothills of the western Caucasus, among the most determined opponents of the Russian conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Abadzekhs inhabited the forested valleys of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belaya-river-kuban">Belaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pshish">Pshish</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psekups">Psekups</a> rivers, behind the coastal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a>. They emerged as a distinct and numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> community of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/circassia">Circassia</a> by the 17th century; earlier history is not separately recorded. Their villages lay scattered in clearings across the forest.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/natukhaj">Natukhaj</a>, they shed the authority of princes in the late 18th century and governed through assemblies and customary law, with sworn associations of households. They lived by farming, herding and forest crafts. From 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-naib-of-shamil">Muhammad Amin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a>'s envoy, organised them into a more centralised Islamic administration with courts and taxes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Abadzekhs submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1859 but soon resumed resistance. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> completed the conquest in 1864, and the great majority were expelled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. A few villages remained near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maykop">Maykop</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adygea">Adygea</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/abadzekhs.jpg" alt="Abadzekhs" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Liptink0 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%90%D0%B1%D0%B4%D0%B7%D0%B0%D1%85_%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%8A_%D0%BB%D1%8A%D1%8D%D0%BF%D0%BA%D1%8A%D1%85%D1%8D%D0%BC_%D1%8F_%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%8B%D0%B3%D1%8A%D1%8D%D1%85%D1%8D%D1%80_001.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Amin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Adyghe (Abadzekh dialect)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abadzekh, Abzakh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=abadzekhs" aria-label="Abadzekhs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5631 211 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Abadzekhs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5955 888h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5951 467h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5951 467h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=abadzekhs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

